#: src/collaboration/channels.md:3
msgid "Channels"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:5
msgid ""
"Channels are persistent rooms for team collaboration. Each channel can "
"contain shared projects, voice chat, and collaborative notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:7
msgid "Channels support:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:9
msgid ""
"Pairing – each collaborator keeps their own screen, mouse, and keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:10
msgid ""
"Mentoring – jump into someone else's context and help without asking them to "
"hand over control."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:11
msgid ""
"Refactoring – multiple people can join the same large refactor in real time."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:12
msgid ""
"Ambient awareness – see what teammates are working on without status "
"meetings."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:14
msgid ""
"Each channel usually maps to an ongoing project or workstream. You can see "
"who's in a channel because their avatars appear in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:17
msgid ""
"Create a channel by clicking the `+` icon next to the `Channels` text in the "
"Collaboration Panel. Create a subchannel by right-clicking an existing "
"channel and selecting `New Subchannel`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:20
msgid ""
"You can keep both work and side-project channels in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:22
msgid ""
"Joining a channel adds you to a shared room where you can work on projects "
"together."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:24
msgid ""
"_Join [our channel tree](https://zed.dev/channel/zed-283) to get an idea of "
"how you can organize yours._"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:26
msgid "Inviting People"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:28
msgid ""
"By default, channels you create can only be accessed by you. You can invite "
"collaborators by right-clicking and selecting `Manage members`."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:31
msgid ""
"When you have subchannels nested under others, permissions are inherited. "
"For instance, adding people to the top-level channel in your channel tree "
"will automatically give them access to its subchannels."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:34
msgid ""
"Once you have added someone, they can either join your channel by clicking "
"on it in their Collaboration Panel, or you can share the link to the channel "
"so that they can join directly."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:36
msgid "Voice Chat"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:38
msgid ""
"You can mute/unmute your microphone via the microphone icon in the upper "
"right-hand side of the window."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:40
msgid ""
"**Note:** When joining a channel, Zed automatically shares your microphone "
"with other users in the call, if your OS allows it. To start muted, use the "
"[`mute_on_join`](../reference/all-settings.md#calls) setting."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:42
msgid "Sharing Projects"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:44
msgid ""
"After joining a channel, you can share a project over the channel via the "
"`Share` button in the upper right-hand side of the window. This will allow "
"channel members to edit the code hosted on your machine as though they had "
"it checked out locally."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:47
msgid ""
"When you edit someone else's project, your editor features still work: jump "
"to definition, use AI features, and view diagnostics. For pairing, one "
"person can implement while the other reads and validates nearby code. "
"Because you keep your own local configuration, the session still feels like "
"your normal setup."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:51
msgid ""
"Collaborators can open, edit, and save files, perform searches, and interact "
"with language servers. Guests have a read-only view of the project, "
"including access to language server info."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:54
msgid "Unsharing a Project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:56
msgid ""
"You can remove a project from a channel by clicking on the `Unshare` button "
"in the title bar."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:58
msgid ""
"Collaborators that are currently in that project will be disconnected from "
"the project and will not be able to rejoin it unless you share it again."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:60
msgid "Channel Notes"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:62
msgid ""
"Each channel has a Markdown notes file associated with it to keep track of "
"current status, new ideas, or to collaborate on building out the design for "
"the feature that you're working on before diving into code."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:64
msgid ""
"This works like a shared Markdown document backed by Zed's collaboration "
"service."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:66
msgid ""
"Open channel notes by clicking the document icon to the right of the channel "
"name in the Collaboration Panel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:68
msgid "**Note:** You can view a channel's notes without joining the channel."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:70
msgid "Following Collaborators"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:72
msgid ""
"To follow a collaborator, click on their avatar in the top left of the title "
"bar. You can also cycle through collaborators using <kbd "
"class=\"keybinding\">ctrl-alt-cmd-f\\|ctrl-alt-shift-f</kbd> or `workspace: "
"follow next collaborator` in the command palette."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:75
msgid ""
"When you join a project, you'll immediately start following the collaborator "
"that invited you."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:77
msgid "When a pane is following a collaborator, it will:"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:79
msgid "follow their cursor and scroll position"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:80
msgid "follow them to other files in the same project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:81
msgid ""
"instantly swap to viewing their screenshare in that pane, if they are "
"sharing their screen and leave the project"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:83
msgid ""
"To stop following, simply move your mouse or make an edit via your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:85
msgid "How Following Works"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:87
msgid ""
"Following is confined to a particular pane. When a pane is following a "
"collaborator, it is outlined in their cursor color."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:90
msgid ""
"Collaborators in the same project appear in color and include a cursor "
"color. Collaborators in other projects are shown in gray."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:93
msgid ""
"This pane-specific behavior allows you to follow someone in one pane while "
"navigating independently in another and can be an effective layout for some "
"collaboration styles."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:95
msgid "Following a Terminal"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:97
msgid ""
"Following in terminals is not currently supported the same way it is in the "
"editor. As a workaround, collaborators can share their screen and you can "
"follow that instead."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:100
msgid "Screen Sharing"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:102
msgid ""
"Share your screen with collaborators in the current channel by clicking on "
"the `Share screen` (monitor icon) button in the top right of the title bar. "
"If you have multiple displays, you can choose which one to share via the "
"chevron to the right of the monitor icon."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:105
msgid ""
"After you've shared your screen, others can click the `Screen` entry under "
"your name in the Collaboration Panel to open a tab that keeps it visible. If "
"they are following you, Zed will automatically switch between following your "
"cursor in their Zed instance and your screen share, depending on whether you "
"are focused on Zed or another application, like a web browser."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:108
msgid ""
"**Warning:** Collaborators can see your entire screen when sharing. Stop "
"screen sharing when finished."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:110
msgid "Livestreaming & Guests"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:112
msgid ""
"A channel can also be made public. This allows anyone to join the channel by "
"clicking on the link."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:115
msgid ""
"Guest users in channels can hear and see everything that is happening, and "
"have read-only access to projects and channel notes."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:117
msgid ""
"If you'd like to invite a guest to participate in a channel for the duration "
"of a call, you can do so by right-clicking them in the Collaboration Panel. "
"\"Allowing Write Access\" will allow them to edit any projects shared into "
"the call, and to use their microphone and share their screen if they wish."
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:120
msgid "Leaving a Call"
msgstr ""

#: src/collaboration/channels.md:122
msgid ""
"You can leave a channel by clicking on the `Leave call` button in the upper "
"right-hand side of the window."
msgstr ""
